<section class="case-sec" id="problem">
  <div>
    <h2>The problem: regressions found by customers</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The worst place to discover a performance regression is in a customer's
        severity-1. The second worst is in a static test plan that ran the same suite
        against every build, regardless of what that build changed — full
        coverage on paper, thin evidence where it mattered.
      </p>
      <p>
        The lab replaced that with an adaptive program: run <strong>thousands of
        controlled simulations per build</strong> across <strong>20 workload types</strong>, treat
        every run as data, and make the next scheduling decision based on what
        the last one found. Before rollout, not after.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="taxonomy">
  <div>
    <h2>From noise to a named taxonomy</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The raw output was unmanageable by design — roughly <strong>500 distinct
        failure signals</strong> thrown off across builds. The analytical work was
        distilling them into <strong>15 recurring failure modes</strong>: named, documented,
        with detection signatures and severity weights. A signal that maps to a
        named mode is a diagnosis; one that doesn't is a discovery, and both are
        useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        Against that taxonomy the program confirmed <strong>50+ real issues</strong> — each
        with reproduction steps and quantified impact, which is the difference
        between a bug report engineering acts on and a graph someone argues
        with.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="bandits">
  <div>
    <h2>Letting bandits overrule the test plan</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        With ~500 signals and finite lab hours, the scheduling question — <em>which
        scenario do we run next?</em> — matters more than any single test. The
        roadmap argued for coverage; the data argued for yield. So scenario
        selection went to <strong>multi-armed bandits</strong>: scenarios that keep finding
        regressions earn more runs, scenarios that stay quiet decay toward a
        baseline exploration rate, and a build that touches a risky subsystem
        re-inflates the arms that historically catch its failure modes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same outcome data tuned the scoring thresholds that decide when a
        signal is worth a human's attention — cutting <strong>false positives by
        60%</strong>. A lab that cries wolf gets ignored by exactly the engineers who
        need to trust its holds.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  </section>

<section class="case-sec decision-sec" id="decision">
  <h2>The decision: let bandits decide what we test next</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The roadmap argued for coverage; the data argued for yield. Thousands of simulations per build across 20 workload types were throwing off ~500 failure signals — far more than a static plan could triage — so I put multi-armed bandits in charge of picking the next scenario and tuned the scoring thresholds on outcomes.</p>
    <p>What it cost: two quarters of a static plan before the scheduler shipped, and a design doc and internal white paper to make the case. What it produced: false positives down 60%, test-infrastructure cost down 40%, and per-build ship/hold calls that prevented 10+ top-severity incidents. Ask me about letting a bandit overrule the test plan.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="shiphold">
  <div>
    <h2>The ship / hold call</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The program's output isn't a dashboard — it's a <strong>decision</strong>. Each build
        gets a verdict assembled from the taxonomy: which failure modes fired,
        at what severity, against which workloads, with what confidence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="table-panel">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>ship-hold ledger — shape of the verdict</span><span class="g">illustrative · identifiers removed</span></div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Build</th><th>Modes fired</th><th>Worst severity</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono dim">build N−2</td><td>none above threshold</td><td class="num">—</td><td><span class="chip pass"><i></i>SHIP</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono dim">build N−1</td><td>latency-degradation under mixed r/w burst</td><td class="num warn">severity-2-class</td><td><span class="chip warn"><i></i>HOLD</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono dim">build N</td><td>same mode, post-fix — regression cleared</td><td class="num">—</td><td><span class="chip pass"><i></i>SHIP</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-foot">a HOLD is a claim with evidence attached: <span class="g">mode, repro steps, quantified impact.</span> that's why holds held.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Cumulative effect: <strong>10+ top-severity customer incidents prevented</strong>, and — because the bandit stopped paying for low-yield runs — <strong>40% lower test-infrastructure cost</strong> —
      top-severity regressions caught in the lab instead of in production. I led
      the two-person effort and wrote the design doc and the internal white
      paper that let the program outlive its authors.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="advisor">
  <div>
    <h2>Shipped in-product: Azure Advisor recommendations</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The same evidence-first muscle shipped customer-facing:
        <strong>15+ Azure Advisor cost and performance recommendations</strong>, built and
        validated with engineering and support, now adopted across
        <strong>hundreds of thousands of customer accounts</strong>. Each recommendation is
        the same artifact as a HOLD verdict — a claim about behavior, backed by
        fleet evidence, precise enough that a customer acts on it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="lessons">
  <h2>What I got wrong</h2>
  <ul class="lessons">
    <li><span class="ed">2026-02</span><span><strong>The static-plan phase ran too long.</strong> The first two quarters scheduled scenarios by roadmap, and quiet arms burned lab hours while risky subsystems went under-tested. The bandit scheduler should have shipped a quarter earlier than it did.</span></li>
  </ul>
</section>

